<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helmuth von Moltke the Elder was a Prussian field marshal and long-serving chief of the Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>. He directed the victories over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1866 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1870–1871, using railways and staff planning to great effect. His successes made possible the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and shaped its military tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helmuth-von-moltke-the-elder.jpg" alt="Helmuth von Moltke the Elder" width="314" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Günther · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Helmuth_von_Moltke_the_Elder_(by_Carl_G%C3%BCnther)_-_BNF_Gallica_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helmuth Karl Bernhard von Moltke was born on 26 October 1800 in Parchim, Mecklenburg-Schwerin, the son of Generalleutnant Friedrich Philipp Victor von Moltke, a German in Danish service. In 1806 French troops burned his father's country house and plundered his townhouse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, leaving the family impoverished. At twelve he entered the Royal Danish Military Academy at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, and in 1818 he became a page to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vi-of-denmark">Frederick VI of Denmark</a> and a second lieutenant. At twenty-one he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> for the Prussian army, despite the loss of seniority, and after the general war school he graduated in 1826.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a young officer Moltke worked on the military survey in Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> and in 1832 was seconded to the general staff at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. He wrote a short romance, The Two Friends, in 1827, and studies of Holland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1835 he took leave to travel in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> asked him to help modernise the Ottoman army. He stayed two years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, learned Turkish and surveyed the city, the Bosporus and the Dardanelles.</p><p>On 29 October 1857 the new king appointed him Chief of the Prussian General Staff, a post he held for thirty years. He changed armament and communications, introduced staff rides, revised mobilisation and began a formal study of European politics for campaign planning. In 1860 he added a Railway Section; a contemporary wrote that he never made an important decision without consulting the railway timetables. He also commanded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870–1871 his plans for a railway deployment south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, revised every year, were adopted without dispute. Five days after the mobilisation order he was made Chief of Staff of the Army for the war, with the right to issue orders equivalent to royal commands. He was made a count in October 1870 after Sedan and a field marshal in June 1871.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Moltke sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> from 1871 to 1891 and oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a>'s official histories of the wars, the history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> appearing between 1874 and 1881. After the war he was a national hero, and more than fifty monuments to him were raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. He married Marie Burt in 1842 and remained devoted to her after her death in 1868. He died on 24 April 1891. He was the earliest-born person whose recorded voice survives: two wax cylinders made on 21 October 1889 hold a line of Hamlet and a passage of Faust.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helmuth von Moltke the Elder” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Prussian General Staff, raised to pre-eminence under <b>Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</b>, made strategic planning a professional discipline.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Helmuth von Moltke the Elder</b>, using railways, the telegraph and the breech-loading needle gun, Prussia defeated Denmark in 1864, Austria at the Battle of Königgrätz in 1866 and France at the Battle of Sedan in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parchim, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.43,11.85&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Parchim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4814 327 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Helmuth von Moltke the Elder"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5264 597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5264 597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.43,11.85&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Eternal peace, however, is a dream, and not even a beautiful dream, for war is part of God's scheme of the world.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Johann Kaspar Bluntschli on the laws of war, 1880 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every law necessitates an authority to watch over it and to direct its execution, but there is no power which can enforce obedience to international agreements.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Johann Kaspar Bluntschli on the laws of war, 1880 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nobody, I think, can deny that the general softening of men's manners has been followed by a more humane way of waging war.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to Johann Kaspar Bluntschli on the laws of war, 1880 &#183; tr. Edmund von Mach, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13056/pg13056-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
